WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.200
Your book came in safely,
with nothing duplicated.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.233
By Monday that file is stale. Keep
the CRM as the record instead.

00:00:07.233 --> 00:00:09.967
GTM OS follows it, writes
back only with approval,

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.267
and can prove nothing went missing.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.100
Over the next few minutes you'll have

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.067
a CRM syncing and a write back approved,

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:20.133
then undone. You'll also build an
adapter for a source that isn't a CRM,

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.433
and check the numbers.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.167
Here's the failure. Leo's mirror
silently drops a slice of the deals,

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.333
and Mia forecasts from what's left.

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.967
Then Orbit writes a score into the
wrong field, and nothing can undo it.

00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:35.567
Both are preventable. Read freely,
write by approval, and prove the count.

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:40.300
First, choose the record, because
everything downstream trusts one CRM.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.867
In the console: Integrations,
Connections, the Composio card.

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.433
Connect Composio once, then connect
your CRM until it shows active.

00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:53.600
HubSpot, Salesforce, Pipedrive, Attio
and Zoho all work through Composio.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.067
Mark the one your team treats
as truth as the CRM of record.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.733
From then on, GTM OS
mirrors your accounts,

00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.800
contacts and deals every six hours.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.567
Sync now runs it on demand,
one at a time per organization.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:08.467
Each record remembers which
CRM record it came from.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:11.567
Mind this: a very large
object can hit a per run cap.

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:14.433
The sync status says so
instead of hiding it.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.633
Or ask your agent: "Set HubSpot
as our CRM of record, sync it,

00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:20.000
and tell me what came in".

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.400
Sign in stays a human click,
so it hands you the link.

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.667
Second, write back, the gated direction.

00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:29.800
Your CRM is the record, so nothing
touches it without a person.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.700
Someone, or an agent, proposes
an update to one record.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.700
That becomes an approval, and
a different person approves it.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.733
The approval is bound to the exact
payload, so what's approved is what runs.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.133
In the console: Operate, Actions.

00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:46.933
Every write back lands there with an
Undo that restores the earlier values.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.633
Two honest limits. Write back
updates records that already exist,

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:52.733
and it can't create one in your CRM.

00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:56.467
And a high risk write through Composio
needs one approval per record,

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:59.933
not per batch. Pro move: write back
only the few fields that matter,

00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:01.600
like scores and outcomes.

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.833
Or ask your agent: "Update
the Northwind deal in HubSpot:

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:08.633
set the next step to send the renewal
quote". It proposes and waits.

00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:11.033
If the value was wrong, ask for the undo.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:14.400
The ledger shows who approved,
and the values before and after.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.833
Third, sources that aren't
a CRM, like a warehouse,

00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:19.600
a billing tool or a niche system.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.100
The adapter builder
maps any list into your

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.033
organization and writes
nothing back to the source.

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:26.600
In the console: CRM, Adapters.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.167
Choose the app or MCP server,
the tool that lists records,

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.600
and the object they fill.

00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:35.533
It starts read only: introspect
pulls one page and lists every field.

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:37.333
Then comes the step most tools skip.

00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:40.533
Run audit counts the records the
source holds against what you mirror,

00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:41.867
before anything is mapped.

00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:45.700
You see the size of the job, and
any gap, while it's cheap to fix.

00:02:45.767 --> 00:02:48.800
Propose mapping drafts every
target field with a confidence bar,

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.667
and shows who decided each line:
a rule, the model, or a person.

00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.533
Anything under sixty percent is flagged.

00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:57.000
Anything ambiguous becomes
a numbered question,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.733
which is also an open ask
a teammate can answer.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.667
Publish proposes a mapping version,
and a person signs it before it runs.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.633
The sync reads only that signed mapping.

00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:09.800
Activate then schedules full reads,
anywhere from every six hours to weekly.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.833
Incremental reads can run as
often as every fifteen minutes,

00:03:12.833 --> 00:03:15.200
if the source can filter by last changed.

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:16.433
Or ask your agent:

00:03:16.433 --> 00:03:19.300
"Build an adapter for the
invoices list in our billing tool.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.300
Audit completeness first and
ask me about anything unclear".

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.833
It stops exactly where
a person has to sign.

00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:29.233
Last move, the proof. Data health asks
one question: is everything there?

00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:31.633
In the console: CRM, Data health.

00:03:31.633 --> 00:03:34.933
Each object shows source rows,
GTM OS rows, rows held back,

00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:37.833
and completeness against your threshold.

00:03:37.833 --> 00:03:41.167
Below it, the object is flagged
and the owner gets a decision.

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:43.533
Agents caveat their answers about it.

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:46.833
Counts can match while values
drift, so check parity.

00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:50.700
It reads a live sample from the
source and compares it with GTM OS,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.500
field by field: identical,
different, or missing.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.933
Operator tip: in Audit settings,
switch on the automatic full sync,

00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:00.233
so a falling object repairs itself.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.367
Or ask your agent: "How
complete is our HubSpot mirror,

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:07.333
and does a live sample match". Owners
and admins can read the parity detail,

00:04:07.333 --> 00:04:11.100
because it carries record values.
Everyone else sees counts only.

00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:15.133
You may be thinking: two copies of my
deals means two versions of the truth.

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:19.133
It doesn't. The CRM stays the record,
and the mirror is measured against it.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:23.267
Completeness runs against your threshold,
and parity compares live values.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.867
Nothing drifts quietly.

00:04:24.933 --> 00:04:29.033
To recap: Pick the record and let
it sync, then gate every write back.

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.200
Audit before you map,
and check parity after.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.500
Read freely, write by
approval, prove the count.

00:04:34.567 --> 00:04:38.500
Your CRM stays the record, and you
can prove the mirror is complete.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.933
Open CRM, then Data health,
and run an audit today.

00:04:41.933 --> 00:04:46.333
The next clip is the CRM workspace,
where every account explains itself.
